schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "Why Regens Are Interested in Blockchain"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: concept
  summary: >-
    An entry-level explainer for permaculturists and community organizers
    brand new to crypto, framing blockchain as plumbing, not magic, and asking
    why a "regen" (regenerative practitioner) would care. Names the fears
    first (scams, energy use, wasting time/looking foolish) and answers each
    honestly. Covers three concrete draws: funding without a gatekeeper
    (quadratic funding via Gitcoin), deciding things together in the open
    (DAOs, Snapshot voting), and ownership that stays local (self-custody,
    tokens as membership/voting markers, Sarafu as the grounded example).
    Closes by citing Greenpill Network's "tool-first honesty" stance: start
    with the community's actual problem, then ask whether blockchain helps.
  audience: permaculturists and community organizers brand new to crypto
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      Tool-first honesty vs. crypto evangelism — the article explicitly
      distances the "regen corner" from broader crypto speculation/scam
      culture, framing usefulness-to-the-work as the only relevant test.
  related_concepts:
    - dao
  related_resources:
    - snapshot
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/why-regens-interested.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

# assessed-but-excluded:
# - Did not create a resource card for Gitcoin here — Gitcoin's quadratic-funding mechanism and
#   figures are captured via a dedicated claim-evidence-style treatment in a related sibling
#   article (why-accept-crypto.md); this article's Gitcoin mention is folded into the summary
#   rather than duplicated as its own object here, to avoid redundant near-identical resource
#   cards across two work orders in the same batch.
